-- title:
--   Surjectivity of Ψ onto Tₚ at the residue characteristic
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a characteristic-zero discrete valuation ring, complete for its maximal-ideal-adic topology, with finite residue field $k=\mathrm{ResidueField}\,\mathcal{O}$, and let $p\neq 2$ be a prime with $p\in\mathfrak m_{\mathcal O}$. Let $\bar\rho$ be a residual Galois representation over $k$: a $2$-dimensional $k$-space $V$ with a monoid homomorphism $\rho$ from $\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb Q}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ to $\mathrm{End}_k V$ trivial on the automorphisms fixing some finite extension of $\mathbb Q$; assume $\bar\rho\otimes_k\bar k$ has no invariant subspace other than $0$ and $V$. Let $S_0\subseteq S$ be finite sets of primes with $p\in S$, and $N\geq 1$ with every prime divisor in $S\setminus S_0$ and $p^2\nmid N$, such that: $\rho$ is trivial on the inertia subgroups at each $q\in S$ with $q\neq p$, $q\nmid N$; for primes $q\neq p$ with $q\,\|\,N$, $\rho$ is non-trivial on some inertia group at $q$ and every inertia element at $q$ has characteristic polynomial $(X-1)^2$; for primes $q\neq p$ with $q^2\mid N$, $q^3\nmid N$ and inertia at $q$ is again unipotent in this sense; and $\bar\rho$ is ordinary at $p$ if $p\mid N$ (vacuous below, since $p\nmid N$ is assumed). Assume the weight-$2$ cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ are $\mathbb C$-spanned by those with integral $q$-expansion coefficients. Let $\theta':\mathbb T^{S_0}\to k$ be a ring homomorphism on the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb C}S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$ generated by the $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S_0$) and the $U_q$ ($q$ prime, $q\mid N$, $q\notin S_0$), subject to: $\mathrm{charpoly}(\rho(\sigma))=X^2-\theta'(T_\ell)X+\ell$ for every $\ell\neq p$ as above and every Frobenius $\sigma$ at $\ell$ for a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\ell$; $\theta'(U_q)=0$ when $q^2\mid N$; and $\theta'(U_q)$ equals the trace of the endomorphism of the inertia coinvariants $V/\sum_{\tau\in I_P}\mathrm{im}(\rho(\tau)-1)$ induced by a Frobenius $\sigma$ at $q$ when $q\,\|\,N$. Assume $p\nmid N$, $p\notin S_0$, and the same trace description for $\theta'(T_p)$ at $p$. Finally let $\theta:\mathbb T^{S}\to k$ be the restriction of $\theta'$ along the inclusion $\mathbb T^{S}\subseteq\mathbb T^{S_0}$, and let $\Psi$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) to [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S₀ 𝒪 θ'`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) (the localisations of the $\mathcal O$-base changes of the two Hecke algebras at the multiplicative sets attached to $\theta$, $\theta'$) commuting with the canonical maps [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.π`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L151) from the Hecke algebras. Then $\pi(T_p)$ in [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S₀ 𝒪 θ'`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) lies in the image of $\Psi$.
--
--   This is the statement that '$T_p$ is redundant' at the residue characteristic when $p\nmid N$: the image of $T_p$ in the local Hecke algebra at $\mathfrak m_{\theta'}$ away from $S_0$ is hit by the comparison map from the local Hecke algebra away from the larger set $S$ (Wiles, Proposition 2.15; Darmon–Diamond–Taylor, Proposition 4.7 in the case of no extra $U$-operator). It is one of the cases feeding the proof that the comparison map between these local Hecke algebras is bijective, and is used in the construction of the Hecke operator $T_p$ inside the localised algebra.
